    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a>, a Sumatran prince who had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a>, founded the city at a fishing village at the mouth of the Malacca River, and Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s protection kept Siam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> from conquering it.</li><li>The Portuguese took it in 1511 and built the fortress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">A Famosa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took it from them in 1641 with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, and in 1824 the British and Dutch agreed that it would pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which handed over in 1825.</li><li>Under the British it was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a>, the Japanese occupied it from 1942 to 1945, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> became independent on 31 August 1957.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to legend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> named the city after the tree under which he was resting when he saw a tiny mouse deer kick his follower's hunting dogs into a river.</p></aside>
    </section>

