    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1396</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> announces that the place will be called Malaka</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1403</span><span class="rf-text">The first official Chinese trade envoy, Admiral Yin Qing, arrives</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1405</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> brings a stone tablet and an imperial order raising the port to a country</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1511</span><span class="rf-text">The Portuguese under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> capture the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1641</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, take Malacca from the Portuguese</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1824</span><span class="rf-text">An Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> treaty places Malacca in British hands, and it is handed over in 1825</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1942</span><span class="rf-text">Japanese forces enter the city and occupy it until 1945</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1957</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> becomes independent on 31 August</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malay-annals.jpg" alt="Malay Annals" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontispiece_of_a_Jawi_edition_of_the_Malay_Annals.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Malacca City stands on both sides of the Malacca River near its mouth on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, on flat and gently undulating land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a>, a Sumatran prince, escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> fell to the Javanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> in 1377 and decided to build a new kingdom. He first settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a>, where he killed Temagi, a regent who served under the Siamese king. Fearing reprisals from Siam, he left, and Temasek was then attacked by Majapahit. At Muar he tried two sites, Biawak Busuk and Kota Buruk, and found them unsuitable. He reportedly visited Sening Ujong before he reached a Malay fishing village at the mouth of the Bertam River, now the Malacca River.</p><p>According to the legend he rested there under a tree called the Malacca tree, and saw a tiny mouse deer kick his follower's hunting dogs into the river. Amused, he decided that the place must be unusual, and in 1396 he announced that it would be called Malaka. Two other accounts of the name exist. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a>, in the Suma Oriental, explained it as a term for a fugitive, reflecting Parameswara's own history, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a> suggest that Arab merchants called the kingdom Malakat, meaning a congregation of merchants, in the reign of Muhammad Shah (1424 to 1444). Under the Portuguese the name was spelled Malaca, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Malakka or Malacka, and under the British Malacca.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ma-huan.jpg" alt="Ma Huan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yingya_Shenglan_(Jilu_Huiban),_first_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ma Huan (馬歡), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The site soon became the centre of the Malay world in the 15th and 16th centuries and the most prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> in the Malay Archipelago. In 1403 the first official Chinese trade envoy, Admiral Yin Qing, arrived, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> was later escorted on his visits by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> and other envoys. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a> and Fei Xin, Malacca had been a relatively insignificant place before these voyages, not even a polity, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Siam. In 1405 the Ming court sent Zheng He with a stone tablet enfeoffing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Western Mountain</a> of Malacca and an imperial order raising the port to the status of a country. The Chinese built a government depot as a fortified cantonment for their soldiers, and Ma Huan reported that Siam did not dare to invade afterwards.</p><p>Ma Huan described a city surrounded by a palisade with four gates and watch towers, and inside it a second fortification holding the merchants' godowns, the treasury and the food storehouses. The river divided the city into two almost equal halves, the south being the inner citadel and the ruler's compound and the north, reached by a bridge, holding the many foreign merchants. About a score of market stalls stood on the bridge, near the docks where foreign ships unloaded.</p><p>Traders specialised by origin: the Gujaratis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> and Bengalis were mostly cloth merchants, the Chinese dealt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> and other islanders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and sandalwood, the Minangkabau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and gold, and the Javanese controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and imported foodstuffs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> founded the city and the Malacca Sultanate. Because of the influence of Arab, Persian and Indian traders it became an Islamic sultanate, and Parameswara converted when he married a princess from Pasai, taking the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Iskandar Shah. He paid tribute in person to the Chinese emperor in 1411 and died in 1414. His son Megat Iskandar Shah succeeded him. Muhammad Shah reigned from 1424 to 1444, Mansur Shah from 1456 to 1477, and Mahmud Shah was the eighth sultan.</p><p>In 1511 the Portuguese took the city. They held it until 1641, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC), which had made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, captured it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> enlarged the fort and built a good deal of new infrastructure, and Malacca was placed under the direct control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. From 1796 to 1801 and from 1807 to 1818 it was placed under a British Resident while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> was under French conquest, and it was returned to the Dutch in 1818.</p><p>A treaty of 1824 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Netherlands led to the city's being handed to the British in 1825, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a> under a Resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>. The Japanese occupied it from 1942 to 1945. It was then administered by the British Military Administration, and then under the Malayan Union and the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. Malaya became independent on 31 August 1957, and the Federation of Malaysia was formed in 1963.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stadthuys"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stadthuys.jpg" alt="Stadthuys" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stadthuys">Stadthuys</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malacca_stadhuys1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">(WT-shared) Slleong at wts wikivoyage, CC BY 1.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city's people have long been of many origins. In the sultanate period a Hindu-Malay and a Tamil-Malay society formed. The Chinese occupied the south-east side of the port around Bukit Cina, where they built temples and a well called Hang Li Poh's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a>. According to local folklore Hang Li Po, a daughter of the Ming emperor, arrived with 500 attendants to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-shah-of-malacca">Sultan Mansur Shah</a>, and the attendants married locals and settled mostly at Bukit Cina. She is also named as the fifth wife of the sixth sultan.</p><p>Because of marriage between communities since the sultanate, the city has its own mixed peoples. The Baba Nyonya are Straits-born Chinese who have lived there for generations as traders and married local Malay women, adopting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-language">Malay language</a> and local culture while keeping some Chinese traditions. The Chitty descend from Indian traders and local women of Malay, Javanese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batak">Batak</a> and Chinese origin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kristang">Kristang</a> descend from Portuguese men and Malay, Chinese and Indian women of the Portuguese period. Malay, Hokkien and English are the main languages, and the Baba Nyonya, Chitty and Kristang each have a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> of their own.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2010 the city had 484,885 people: 273,844 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, 158,828 Chinese, 20,310 Indians, 9,732 others and about 22,171 non-citizens. Most Malays were Muslims, the Chinese mostly Buddhists, Confucianists, Taoists or followers of Chinese folk religion, the Indians mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and the Kristang mostly Christians. The Portuguese built churches, convents and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s palace, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Square holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stadthuys">Stadthuys</a> and Christ Church.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/a-famosa.jpg" alt="A Famosa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">A Famosa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Famosa_Fortress.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chongkian, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Portuguese came for the trade. Their envoy Diogo Lopes de Sequeira was first well received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mahmud Shah, but the Tamil Muslim community, citing the Portuguese treatment of the Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, persuaded the sultan to act against them, and he had several of the delegation captured and killed. In April 1511 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> arrived with an armada. He wished to cut off Islamic and Venetian trade, and he said that if Malacca were taken out of the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> would be ruined. A first attack on 25 July 1511 failed, and a second succeeded in August 1511.</p><p>The Portuguese built the fortress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">A Famosa</a> from stone taken from Muslim graves, mosques and other buildings. They imposed higher taxes on Chinese traders and restricted their ownership of land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> then fought each other and the Portuguese for the city, in attacks in 1564, 1570, 1613 and 1623 and a triangular war, until a large armada from Goa came to defend Malacca and destroyed the sultanate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Johor then took the city from the Portuguese in 1641.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, news of the sinking of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> and Repulse on 10 December 1941 caused panic, British officials fled, and thousands of residents hid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> estates and jungle. The Japanese army arrived on 14 January 1942 and there was no major battle. The Kempeitai used the former Government Rest House for arrests, torture and executions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> rations were low, and a number of residents were taken to build the Burma-Siam Railway. The city was left undamaged when Japan surrendered in August 1945.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman.jpg" alt="Tunku Abdul Rahman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tunku_abd_rahman_(cropped,_4to3_port,_bypass).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and British each left marks on the city. The Dutch Square is surrounded by Dutch buildings such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stadthuys">Stadthuys</a> and Christ Church, and the Portuguese traces are mostly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">Porta de Santiago</a> and St Peter's Church. The British dismantled the old fort and made rules for infrastructure that produced back alleys, back yards, fire escapes and pedestrian arcades. The historic central area lies near the old coastline, with St Paul's Hill and the ruins of the Portuguese fortress and the Dutch Square on the eastern bank of the river and the old Chinatown on the western.</p><p>The communities that grew from the city's history keep their traditions: the Peranakan Chinese in Chinatown, the Chitty and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kristang">Kristang</a>, whose Portuguese Settlement is the centre of Portuguese culture, with many Eurasians descended from Portuguese men and local women. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> Cenotaph on Bukit Cina commemorates thousands of Chinese in Malacca who were killed by the Japanese during the occupation. The Padang Pahlawan is the site where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a> made his first announcement of independence.</p><p>Malacca was given the title of Historical City on 15 April 1989, was granted city status on 15 April 2003, and on 7 July 2008 was listed, with George Town in the north of the peninsula, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Its council area was set at 270 square kilometres after land reclamation carried out since 1974, and the city today is the political and economic centre of the state of Malacca.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Malacca City, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, was founded by the Sumatran prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> in 1396, and became a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> protected by Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Portuguese took it in 1511, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1641 and the British in 1825. It is the capital of the Malaysian state of Malacca.</em></p>

